From: u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com (Dennis Murphy)
Date: Thu, 18 Apr 2013 07:02:18 +0000
Subject: Re: factors on x-axis
Message-ID: <al7mSASDG60.5NS02S9ziC8J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <al7mSASDG60.rElFEEO96UEJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <al7mSASDG60.eIRJikiEaxcJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com> <al7mSASDG60.rElFEEO96UEJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/al7mSASDG60/m/5NS02S9ziC8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I presume there's a reason behind why you're rbinding data frames
together and using aes_string(), so we'll play along. Thanks for the
reproducible example. This note is to try to summarize Kaori's and
Ista's contributions.

Your first example worked because each variable contained elements
having the same class; in other words, your data frame is basically a
list of atomic vectors, each of which has the same length but possibly
different class. Your second example initially failed because "V" is
character. When added to a numeric (atomic) vector, it coerces its
class to one that can reconcile both types - either character or
factor would have sufficed in this instance.
> class(seq(5))
[1] "integer"
> class(c(seq(5), "V"))
[1] "character"
Inside a data frame, the default behavior (often annoying) is to
convert strings to factors, which is why this became a factor in the
data frame.

Once x became a factor, it lost its numeric characteristics, so by
default geom_line() will not render the geom unless a suitable group =
specification is given. Had no aesthetics been assigned to the lines,
group = 1 probably would have done the job, but since you did, your
grouping has to reflect it. As Ista correctly observed, creating an
interaction between g and l is the way to go, and it has to be done
outside of ggplot2 since it won't let you create it on the fly inside
a ggplot() specification:

> ggplot(d) + geom_line(aes_string(x = "x", y = "y", color = "g",
                                 linetype = "l",
                                 group = interaction("g", "l"))
Error: geom_path: If you are using dotted or dashed lines, colour, size
and linetype must be constant over the line

As a result, either of these will create the necessary interaction:

d$gp <- with(d, interaction(g, l))
d$group <- with(d, paste0(g, l))

and now either of the following will work:

ggplot(d) + geom_line(aes_string(x = "x", y = "y", color = "g",
                                 linetype = "l", group = "gp"))
ggplot(d) + geom_line(aes_string(x = "x", y = "y", color = "g",
                                 linetype = "l", group = "group"))

Hope this aids in understanding.
Dennis

